 (:adr/body "Closing record for the 2026-09-09/10 session. What landed, what is verified,
what is blocked and whose call it is, and the one command that resumes.

## What landed, all confirmed on the default branch

  amu#912          9ee8020b   a module declaring :schemas can be required
  kiyaku#2         3e28dcdc   postal_core requires pattern-vm, owns valid-postal?
  pattern#1        9bd80eda   captures, opcode 5 save
  pattern#2        08029d3b   expressible, the goal-level measurement, ratcheted
  pattern#3        26ef01e3   `.` excludes line terminators; (?i)(?s)(?m)
  pattern#4        0da273cc   lookahead (?=) (?!)
  pattern          c80d5ed0   find-from, and the empty-match hazard
  amu#917          2abd9843   value-bounds agreement guard, nine pairs
  osaho#88         7a6a27b8   document-node-limit 256 -> 4096, with a derivation
  kotoba-script#101 b4099699  max-document-nodes, its restatement
  amu#918          efac8a3d   both pins advanced together
  amu#919          8abb8d5f   the todo example uncapped
  htmldom          c6235eb3   dom encoding probe
  htmldom          2ed8ff55   kotoba/dom_table
  htmldom          4d8bb60a   kotoba/entity_table + generator
  htmldom          68752e72   kotoba/numeric_ref

Ancestry checked with `gh api compare/<sha>...main` rather than assumed. All
six west pins equal their repo main: amu, pattern, htmldom, osaho,
kotoba-script, kiyaku.

Also filed: kotoba-lang/amu#915 (open, a recursion group of exactly 8 trivial
members or 7 real ones blows the compiler heap). Closed: kotoba-sema#78, which
was filed against the wrong repo.

## Verification, with its limits stated

  amu               1372 tests / 10381 assertions   0 fail   + 13/13 CI checks
  pattern           vm 320 · pattern 284 · emit 168 · compile 157 · capture 1278
  kotoba-script     68 / 248                        0 fail
  osaho             355 / 2325                      1 fail (pre-existing)
  htmldom           dom-table 5 · entity 5+89 · numeric 6+10960

osaho's one failure is `the-native-gate-still-refuses-what-it-always-refused`,
red on that repo main for three merges BEFORE this session, with before/after
baselines identical. Not caused here, and merged through consistent with that
repo's own recent practice -- which is itself worth someone deciding
deliberately rather than by habit.

⚠ One evidence limit is structural and was stated at the time rather than
discovered later: raising a ceiling cannot break tests whose fixtures sit below
it, so \"no regressions\" on the node-budget change is guaranteed rather than
earned. What made the check non-vacuous was the other direction -- lowering the
limit to 4 produces 7 errors.

## The four ceilings

  256-node budget      7 rows       a number nobody derived
  4096-node budget     26           the example's alphabet cap
  integer item keys    27           fuel, at the default 512
  fuel >= ~100k        32           document-container-item-limit

Three removed. The fourth is the first with a derivation and should NOT be
raised: with depth 8, 32-per-container gives 32^8 by nesting. A screen is
naturally flat; a model is not.

## Blocked, and whose call

  1. NON-GREEDY is a semantics decision, not an implementation task, and is
     the one item that needs an owner. Under leftmost-longest `*?` and `*`
     cannot differ. Supporting it means either giving the VM priority
     semantics -- which changes every answer the parity suites currently pin,
     including the POSIX/Perl divergence probe -- or refusing it permanently
     and rewriting three cssom patterns (`/\\*.*?\\*/` becomes
     `/\\*([^*]|\\*[^/])*\\*/`). Deliberately not started.

  2. LOOKBEHIND waits on amu#915. `test/lookbehind_probe.cljs` compiles the
     module that would ship and EXPECTS FAILURE, so the block lifts itself.

  3. The shared amu checkout is behind its pin with a live writer in it. Its
     local-only commit is preserved as `refs/preserved/amu-rank-tick-355` and
     its working tree archived under `.git/stash-archive-20260909/`. One
     `west update --fetch smart amu` once that bot is idle. NOT this session's
     to stash.

## Resume point

htmldom is NOT migrated, and calling it so would be the decision-only slice
ADR-q9 forbids. Five public functions, all still `.cljc`: `decode-entities`,
`tokenize`, `parse-style`, `style-importance`, `parse-into-document`.

Every piece the FIRST of them needs now exists and is measured: the entity
table (89/89), numeric references (10960/10960), the regex it uses (15/15
against JavaScript), and scanning from a position. The next step is to assemble
`decode-entities` and run it against the `.cljc`, which is the first whole
public function in this arc with an oracle rather than a table.

  cd orgs/kotoba-lang/htmldom
  sed -n '222,275p' src/htmldom/core.cljc        # the contract
  nbb docs/dom_encoding_probe.cljs               # the shape decisions, re-measured

Two rules this session earned the hard way, for whoever picks it up:

  * An imported function is called DIRECTLY -- `(data/table)` -- while a
    function of the same module goes through `(app (ref f) ...)`.
  * There is no `string<?` and no `i64-from-string`. `entity_table` carries
    its own ordering; a decimal parse is `numeric_ref/parse-digits`.

## What this session got wrong

Six statements that were not facts, five found in existing work and ONE
propagated by me into an ADR an hour after writing that this is the failure
mode to watch for. Recorded in adr-2609100200.

Three counting errors, each caught by a second measurement rather than by
reading: a loop count read as a row count (400 vs a 423-character render), a
file-wide tag counter read as per-case (102 vs 6), and an ancestry filter
written backwards during this very closing, which reported ten landed merges as
NOT ON MAIN before the condition was corrected.

Two discrimination attempts rejected for being red for the wrong reason: one
crashed instead of disagreeing, and one used a sweep abbreviated below the
range it was meant to test -- reporting 8704/8704 green against a defect it
could not reach.

Two failures a local suite structurally could not see, both landed by CI and
both mine: `deps-lock.edn` is read only by the JVM-free path, and the Playwright
spec is run only by CI.

And one misattribution: browser-matrix had been red on main since an earlier
merge, and the timeline said that merge caused it. The LOG said chrome failed
to install. Two reds adjacent in a history are not the same red.")))
